{
  "dom_id": "step-33806",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Visually Identifying Convex Polyhedra",
  "problem": "Which of the polyhedra below are convex?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268220/assets/dd92448e24dc4d849b66b156702789f518549db5bdcb92fac895ea3e53c676cf.png)",
  "worked_solution": "A polyhedron is convex if and only if the following condition holds:\n\n*If we take any two distinct points $A$ and $B$ that belong to the polyhedron, then all the points on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ must also belong to the polyhedron.*  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our solids in turn.\n\n- Solid I is *not* convex. The above condition does not hold. As a counterexample, let's consider the points $A$ and $B,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14268220/assets/4efd455e4761b5e47835f9689de2f095898055410549f43618cc648e74ed07f5.png)\n\nNotice that some points on the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ lie outside the solid.\n\n- Solids II and III are convex. Indeed, the above condition holds for any two points that belong to the solids.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
